# ============================ ACCOUNTS 7 ============================
LESSONS_A7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The monthly close in ZhiftERP", "11", "<p>This module covers the period close and the financial statements: the close checklist, the cut-off reports, the three statement reports and how to read them, the Asset module, and year-end with the Period Closing Voucher.</p><p><b>What closing a month means in the system.</b> A month is closed when:</p><p>1. All its documents are submitted (cut-off — Chapter 3's reports find the stragglers).<br>2. The recurring Journal Entries have run and the month's journals are reviewed (module 2).<br>3. The standing checks have passed — reconciliations, bridges, ties (Chapter 2 lists them in order).<br>4. The Trial Balance has been scanned and its anomalies worked (module 1).<br>5. The period is locked: <b>Accounts Settings &gt; Accounts Frozen Upto</b> is advanced to the month's end date, and the matching stock freeze (Stock Settings &gt; Stock Frozen Upto) moves with it. From that moment, postings dated inside the sealed month are refused for everyone except the holders of the <b>Role Allowed to Set Frozen Accounts &amp; Edit Frozen Entries</b> — a role granted to two named people at most (module 8).</p><p>A closed month's statements can be circulated without qualification, and they stay as reported: corrections discovered later post in the current month under module 2's rules.</p><p><b>Why monthly.</b> Closing once a year produces the January backlog: twelve months of unsubmitted drafts, unswept suspense, unreconciled banks, and unaccrued costs, processed months after the events. Closing monthly keeps each month's work small because the previous month's was done, catches errors while the documents and the people are fresh, and turns year-end into an ordinary close plus a short annual list (Chapter 8). The first monthly close carries the backlog and takes days; the sixth takes hours. The standing target: <b>day five</b> — sealed and read by the fifth working day.</p><p><b>The three statement reports.</b> All under Accounting &gt; Financial Statements, all produced from the sealed month:</p><p>• <b>Profit and Loss Statement</b> — what the period earned and consumed (Chapter 4).<br>• <b>Balance Sheet</b> — what the business owns and owes at the sealed date (Chapter 5).<br>• <b>Cash Flow</b> — where the money went (Chapter 6).</p><p>All three share the same filters — Company, period, periodicity, Cost Center — and every figure drills to the General Ledger on click.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the close checklist. Chapter 3: cut-off and its reports. Chapters 4-6: reading the three statements. Chapter 7: the Asset module. Chapter 8: year-end, the audit, and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The close checklist", "12", "<p>The close runs from one checklist: nine lines, each with a named owner, in order — later lines verify earlier ones.</p><p><b>1. Cut-off sweep.</b> The month's documents in: draft documents submitted or cancelled, the <b>Delivered Items To Be Billed</b> and <b>Received Items To Be Billed</b> reports read in naira, known latecomers accrued. Chapter 3 covers the reports.</p><p><b>2. The manual layer.</b> The recurring set runs — the Auto Repeat entries confirmed fired, the template journals posted (accruals, prepayment consumptions, the salary posting, depreciation where not yet automated); accrual reversals confirmed (the Reverse Journal Entry pairs); the month's journal review from the filtered Journal Entry list — the five questions, the count.</p><p><b>3. The sweeps.</b> Suspense to zero, each item classified. The unaddressed check — the ledger filtered to the Company's default cost centre — at or near zero (module 6). Floating payments and credits cleared in Payment Reconciliation (module 4).</p><p><b>4. The reconciliations.</b> Every bank matched in the Bank Reconciliation Tool; the Bank Reconciliation Statement saved per account; cash counts current (module 3).</p><p><b>5. The bridges.</b> Stock ledger to stock accounts. Sales Register and Purchase Register tax totals to the tax accounts' GL movements (module 5's pre-filing gate, run whether or not a filing falls this week). Trial Balance controls to the Accounts Receivable and Payable report totals (module 4's tie check).</p><p><b>6. Subsidiary outputs collected.</b> The AR/AP routine's artifacts, the tax cycles' zero checks, the stock month-end outputs — collected, not re-performed.</p><p><b>7. The Trial Balance scan.</b> Module 1's ten minutes: should-be-zero accounts with balances (Stock Received But Not Billed aging, suspense residents, the Round Off account trending), wrong-side balances, unrecognised lines — each opened in the General Ledger report.</p><p><b>8. The freeze advance.</b> Only after lines 1-7: <b>Accounts Frozen Upto</b> advanced to the month's end date, the stock freeze with it, the advance noted in the close log. The order is fixed — sweep first, freeze second; a close that freezes first manufactures its own next-month stragglers.</p><p><b>9. Statements produced and read.</b> The three reports run from the sealed month, the twenty-minute management reading held (Chapters 4-6), and the close bundle filed: the signed checklist, the reconciliation statements, the bridge confirmations, the reports — one folder per month.</p><p>Practical rules: one owner for the checklist, a named owner per line, lines 1-8 in days one to four, line 9 on day five. A line that keeps slipping is a process fault to fix, not a person to blame.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Cut-off and its reports", "12", "<p>Cut-off is the rule that a month contains exactly its own documents. Its failures move real margin between months through omission alone, and the system has reports built for catching them.</p><p><b>The completeness pairs, with their reports.</b></p><p>• <b>Deliveries and their invoices.</b> Revenue books at the Sales Invoice; COGS books at the Delivery Note. A month whose deliveries posted but whose invoices lagged carries cost without revenue. The report: <b>Delivered Items To Be Billed</b> (Accounting or Selling reports) — every delivery awaiting its invoice, in naira. At close, the queue is either billed or carried knowingly with a note.<br>• <b>Receipts and their bills.</b> The mirror report: <b>Received Items To Be Billed</b>. The accounting side is handled by design — the Purchase Receipt credits <b>Stock Received But Not Billed</b>, so the liability accrues whether or not the supplier's invoice arrived. The close's job is the <b>SRBNB age read</b>: an old, large balance on that account means purchase invoices are not being processed.<br>• <b>Consumptions and their accruals.</b> Utilities consumed but unbilled, services received but uninvoiced — covered by the accrual templates in checklist line 2 (module 2's estimate → Reverse Journal Entry → actual rhythm).</p><p><b>The document sweep, step by step.</b></p><p>1. For each transaction doctype (Sales Invoice, Delivery Note, Purchase Receipt, Purchase Invoice, Payment Entry, Stock Entry, Journal Entry), open the list view filtered to <b>Status = Draft</b> and dated this month. This five-minute pass finds most stragglers — submit or cancel each one.<br>2. Read the two To-Be-Billed reports and act on them.<br>3. Accrue the known latecomers — the supplier who always bills on the 5th for the prior month gets a template accrual, reversed on arrival.<br>4. Run the sweep on <b>day one</b> of the close, so stragglers post with honest in-period dates before the freeze advances.</p><p><b>Worked example — why one document swings two months.</b> A ₦500,000 delivery posts on the 30th; its invoice posts on the 2nd. Month one records ₦380,000 of COGS with no revenue: margin understated by ₦120,000. Month two records ₦500,000 of revenue with no COGS: margin overstated by ₦120,000. One late invoice, two distorted months in opposite directions — which is why a gross margin that swings on stable trade points first to cut-off, and why the To-Be-Billed reports are read at every close.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Check the pairs with their reports — Delivered Items To Be Billed, Received Items To Be Billed, the SRBNB age; sweep drafts by list view on day one; accrue the latecomers; freeze only after.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Reading the Profit and Loss Statement", "12", "<p>Open <b>Accounting &gt; Financial Statements &gt; Profit and Loss Statement</b>. Set Company, the period, and Periodicity = Monthly with several months of columns — the trend view is the report's most useful shape. This chapter is the structure, the reading method, and the pre-reading checks.</p><p><b>The structure, top to bottom.</b></p><p>• <b>Income</b> — the period's invoiced trade, net of returns and credit notes.<br>• <b>Cost of Goods Sold</b> — the delivered goods' valuation, summed from the stock postings.<br>• <b>Gross Profit</b> — read with its <b>percentage</b>: the trading engine's health in one number, and the first line every reader checks.<br>• <b>Expenses</b> — grouped as the chart's design intended them to be read (module 1's reading test paying off monthly).<br>• <b>Net Profit</b> — after finance costs, exceptional items (labelled as exceptional — one blended into operations distorts every trend), and the business's own tax charge.</p><p><b>The reading method — four rules, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>Trend before level.</b> Run with monthly columns and read this month against the last six. A gross margin that moved from 24% to 21% over a quarter is a finding regardless of whether 21% is acceptable.<br>2. <b>Rate before naira.</b> Margins and expense ratios compare across months of different sizes; raw naira mislead.<br>3. <b>Decompose before concluding.</b> A falling margin splits three ways — price, mix, or cost — with three different owners. Re-run the report filtered by <b>Cost Center</b> (and by the line dimension where module 6 added one): margin by branch and by line shows which component moved.<br>4. <b>Drill before disputing.</b> Click any figure: the report opens the General Ledger behind it, and the vouchers are two more clicks away. The disputed expense is opened live in the meeting, not argued from the page.</p><p><b>The pre-reading checks.</b> Before trusting the figures, confirm the month's disciplines held:</p><p>• Cut-off — the To-Be-Billed reports were read (Chapter 3); the usual cause of a bouncing margin.<br>• Valuation — a COGS jump traced to a bad incoming rate; check the item's rate history.<br>• Accruals — the \"cheap\" month that simply has not been billed yet (line 2 ran).<br>• The unaddressed check — branch reads distorted by blank cost centres (module 6).<br>• Exceptional labelling — one-offs marked as one-offs.</p><p><b>The management reading.</b> Twenty minutes, monthly, on a fixed day: the three-number headline (revenue, gross margin percentage, net profit), the trend columns, the material variances with one-line causes, and any decision the figures require. A standing meeting, not an email.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Reading the Balance Sheet", "12", "<p>Open <b>Accounting &gt; Financial Statements &gt; Balance Sheet</b> as at the sealed date. The total always balances — the posting engine guarantees it — so the reading is line by line: every line is a claim, and every claim has a check performed elsewhere in this track.</p><p><b>The line checks.</b></p><p>• <b>Fixed assets</b> — cost less accumulated depreciation, agreeing with the Asset module's register (Chapter 7); the last physical verification current.<br>• <b>Stock</b> — the stock-to-accounts bridge agreed at close; the valuation honest; the dead-stock and aging reads qualifying how good the asset is beyond how big.<br>• <b>Debtors</b> — gross less the Provision for Doubtful Debts, shown rather than hidden; the tie check agreed (Trial Balance control = Accounts Receivable report total); the aging supporting the provision (module 4's matrix).<br>• <b>Prepayments and supplier advances</b> — the Prepaid Rent consuming on its Auto Repeat schedule; the supplier advances aged against expected deliveries, clean of items past plausibility.<br>• <b>WHT Receivable</b> — certificated or under active chase; the asset is only as real as its certificate file (module 5).<br>• <b>Bank and cash</b> — every balance matched in the Bank Reconciliation Tool this month, the Bank Reconciliation Statements in the close bundle, the floats counted.<br>• <b>Creditors</b> — tied to the Accounts Payable report; the SRBNB age read; supplier statements reconciled for the top names.<br>• <b>Customer advances and accrued expenses</b> — reported un-netted, each with its schedule; these liabilities are what keep the P&amp;L honest.<br>• <b>Tax custodies</b> — at their explainable levels (post-remittance zero or the stated carry-forward), the reserve funded to match (module 5).<br>• <b>Equity</b> — retained earnings moves only through the <b>Period Closing Voucher</b> (Chapter 8) and governed distributions. An unexplained equity movement is a serious finding: deleted documents, forced balances, and migration errors all surface in equity, because equity is the residual where broken books hide. Chapter 9's Pattern 5.</p><p><b>The monthly balance-sheet read, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. Run the report beside last month's (Periodicity = Monthly, two columns).<br>2. Read the working-capital lines against last month — stock, debtors, creditors; Chapter 6 restates these movements as cash.<br>3. Confirm the custody levels and the equity line unchanged except by known events.<br>4. Any line whose check failed is worked before the statements circulate, or circulated with an explicit note — never silently.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Reading the Cash Flow statement", "12", "<p>Open <b>Accounting &gt; Financial Statements &gt; Cash Flow</b>. This report reconciles two facts that regularly diverge: the P&amp;L shows a profit while the bank falls, or the reverse. Most trading businesses never read theirs.</p><p><b>Why profit and cash differ.</b> Profit records earnings — a submitted Sales Invoice is revenue whether or not the customer has paid. Cash records movements. The gap is the balance sheet moving:</p><p>• Profit that became <b>stock</b> — margin sitting in the warehouse.<br>• Profit that became <b>debtors</b> — margin sitting in unpaid invoices.<br>• Profit consumed by <b>paying creditors down</b>.<br>• Profit spent on <b>assets</b> — the cold room capitalised to the balance sheet, not expensed.</p><p>A profitable business can run out of cash; a cash-rich business can be quietly unprofitable. The report starts from profit and shows, movement by movement, where the money went.</p><p><b>The three sections, as the report presents them.</b></p><p>1. <b>Operating activities</b> — profit adjusted for non-cash lines (depreciation added back: it consumed no cash) and the working-capital movements (stock up consumes cash; debtors up consumes cash; creditors up releases it). A healthy trading business generates positive operating cash most months. Chronically negative operating cash at a profitable business means the margin is being buried in stock and debtors faster than it is earned — the figure that explains why growth \"doesn't feel like it\".<br>2. <b>Investing activities</b> — the asset spend: the Chapter 7 capitalisations landing as outflows.<br>3. <b>Financing activities</b> — capital in, loans drawn and repaid, distributions out.</p><p>The three sections sum to the period's net cash movement, which agrees with the bank accounts' movement — already proven line by line in module 3's reconciliations.</p><p><b>The five-minute monthly read.</b> The formal report exists; the working read is four questions:</p><p>1. Was operating cash positive — did the engine generate or consume?<br>2. Which working-capital line moved most, and why — one sentence per material movement, drilled from the Balance Sheet comparison?<br>3. What was invested, and was it planned?<br>4. What financed the difference, and is that source sustainable?</p><p>Practical rule: when the P&amp;L and the bank app appear to disagree, do not argue from either — open the Cash Flow report. The answer is always one of the working-capital lines, and the report names which one moved.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The Asset module: capitalisation to disposal", "13", "<p>Purchases that outlive their month — vehicles, generators, cold rooms, fit-outs — run through the Asset module, which maintains the register, computes depreciation, and posts it automatically.</p><p><b>Setup — the Asset Category.</b> Open <b>Assets &gt; Asset Category &gt; New</b>: name the class (Vehicles, Plant, Fit-outs), set its accounts per company (Fixed Asset account, Accumulated Depreciation, Depreciation Expense), and its depreciation defaults — method (<b>Straight Line</b> is the trading business's standard), total number of depreciations, and frequency in months. The category is the policy carrier; individual assets inherit it.</p><p><b>The capitalisation policy.</b> Written, with a value threshold: below it, expense regardless — a ₦40,000 printer is not worth an asset record's lifetime of administration. Above it, capitalise into the category list. The threshold is policy, not mood: expensing everything to dampen a good year, or capitalising to flatter a bad one, is the same abuse as moving a provision by discretion.</p><p><b>Creating assets from their documents.</b> On the <b>Item record</b>, tick <b>Is Fixed Asset</b> and set the Asset Category. A Purchase Receipt or Purchase Invoice for that item then creates the <b>Asset record</b> automatically — the asset born from its purchase document, not reconstructed from a year-end spreadsheet. On the Asset record, confirm: gross purchase amount, <b>Available-for-Use Date</b> (depreciation starts here, not at the invoice date), location, and <b>Cost Center</b> — so depreciation lands on the branch that uses the asset. Submitting the Asset generates its <b>depreciation schedule</b> — the table of dates and amounts for its whole life.</p><p><b>Monthly depreciation.</b> With <b>Accounts Settings &gt; Post Depreciation Entries Automatically</b> enabled, the system posts each schedule row on its date: debit Depreciation Expense (addressed by the asset's cost centre), credit Accumulated Depreciation. The Balance Sheet carries cost less accumulated depreciation as net book value. Verify the posting once against the map; after that it is checklist line 2 confirming it fired.</p><p><b>Disposals.</b> When an asset is sold or scrapped, use the Asset record's <b>Sell / Scrap</b> actions: selling creates the Sales Invoice against the asset (proceeds versus net book value, gain or loss posted); scrapping posts the write-off Journal Entry. Either way the Asset record's status closes with its date and evidence. An asset that physically left without a disposal entry leaves the register asserting property the business no longer holds. Location moves between branches are recorded with <b>Asset Movement</b>.</p><p><b>The annual verification.</b> Run the <b>Fixed Asset Register</b> report and physically verify it once a year — each asset sighted or its absence resolved through the disposal flow, locations and cost centres corrected, the verification signed and filed. The Balance Sheet's fixed-asset line is only as reliable as the last verification.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Year-end, the audit, and the HealthTrade case", "13", "<p>Year-end is the twelfth monthly close plus a short annual list. This chapter covers the mechanics, the audit, and HealthTrade's close year.</p><p><b>The year-end additions.</b></p><p>1. <b>The Period Closing Voucher.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Period Closing Voucher &gt; New</b>: select the fiscal year, the closing account (Retained Earnings), and the closing cost centre, and submit. The voucher closes every P&amp;L account to retained earnings in one governed posting — the year's result becoming equity's newest layer, and the <b>only routine way retained earnings ever moves</b>.<br>2. <b>The Fiscal Year.</b> The next year created at least two months early (Accounting &gt; Fiscal Year — module 8 covers the doctype), so the January 1st document never finds its year missing.<br>3. <b>The annual layer landing together, scheduled across weeks:</b> the full stock count, the Fixed Asset Register verification (Chapter 7), the chart and settings walks, module 5's annual returns.<br>4. <b>The audit file</b> — for a monthly-closing business, the year's twelve close bundles plus the annual artifacts, assembled by collation rather than creation.</p><p><b>The external audit, met.</b> The interim walk-through tests the system of control — the tie, the bridges, the reconciliations, the separations — and a business that passes earns sampling instead of full substantive testing, which is the audit-fee conversation in one sentence. Fieldwork drills samples to documents (the drill-down performing for its most demanding audience). Confirmations arrive pre-answered by module 4's hygiene. The auditor's proposed adjustments — there will be some — are agreed or contested professionally by the accountant and <b>booked by module 2's rules</b>: referenced entries in the current or formally reopened period, never quiet edits, so the audited statements and the ledger end the season as the same document.</p><p><b>HealthTrade's close year.</b> The first close takes four days of backlog: the draft sweep finds <b>eleven stragglers</b> across the doctypes, suspense empties (<b>₦93,000 across six items</b>), the To-Be-Billed reports are read for the first time, and the checklist runs to signature. Month two closes in two days. By month five the close lands on <b>day three</b>, and the statements are read at the day-five standing meeting. The readings produce decisions within the quarter: the Cash Flow read exposes the growth squeeze — positive profit, negative operating cash from stock build — which triggers the purchasing review; the P&amp;L trend columns catch a two-point margin slide, decomposed by cost centre to one branch's discounting; and the Balance Sheet walk retires the last unexplained line. At year-end: the Period Closing Voucher posts retained earnings' only movement of the year, the audit's walk-through passes on first presentation, fieldwork samples rather than excavates, and the three agreed adjustments book by the rules. The audit closes three weeks earlier than the prior year, at a lower fee, with the statements and the ledger identical.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "11", "<p>The close-and-statements layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the never-closed month.</b> Symptom: Accounts Frozen Upto blank or a year old; statement dates nobody trusts; every report a draft. Cause: the close never instituted (Chapters 1-2). Fix: the first close performed once with its backlog, then the checklist with named owners, the day-five target, and the freeze advance keeping each sealed month sealed.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the year-end backlog.</b> Symptom: twelve months of accruals, reconciliations, and corrections processed in January; statements about a year nobody remembers. Cause: closing annually what should close monthly (Chapter 1). Fix: the monthly rhythm — the pattern disappears by construction.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the bouncing margin.</b> Symptom: gross margin swinging month to month on stable trade; owners distrusting the P&amp;L. Cause: cut-off noise read as news (Chapter 3). Fix: the day-one draft sweep, the Delivered and Received Items To Be Billed reports read in naira at every close, the SRBNB age watched, and the ₦500,000 worked example taught to whoever reads the statements.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — statements produced but not read.</b> Symptom: the reports generated, circulated, filed — and no decision ever traced to them; the owner learning the business's position from the bank app. Cause: the reading habit never installed (Chapters 4-6). Fix: the twenty-minute monthly reading as a standing meeting, the four P&amp;L rules, the balance-sheet line checks, the four cash-flow questions, and the three-number headline.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unexplained equity movement.</b> Symptom: retained earnings shifting between periods with no Period Closing Voucher behind it; equity used as a plug. Cause: the most serious failure here — history edited at its last hiding place (Chapters 5, 8): deleted documents, forced balances, migration errors, all washing up in equity. Fix: each movement traced in the General Ledger to its cause, the Period Closing Voucher restored as retained earnings' only routine mover, deletion rights removed (module 8's settings), and the equity line added to every monthly balance-sheet walk.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the five conditions of a closed month and where the freeze lives (Accounts Frozen Upto + the frozen-edit role); run the nine checklist lines in order and explain sweep-before-freeze; use the two To-Be-Billed reports and the SRBNB age read, and walk the ₦500,000 example; read the P&amp;L with the four rules and the pre-reading checks; walk the Balance Sheet line checks and state the equity rule; answer the Cash Flow's four questions; set up an Asset Category, capitalise from a purchase document with Is Fixed Asset, enable automatic depreciation, and run a disposal; and post a Period Closing Voucher and assemble the audit file by collation.</p><p><b>The seventh law: the books close monthly, the statements are read monthly, and a sealed month stays sealed.</b> Module 8 covers the settings, authorities, and calendar that hold the finance layer together.</p>"),
]

