# ============================ ACCOUNTS 8 ============================
LESSONS_A8 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Accounts Settings walk", "12", "<p>This final module covers the structural layer: the Accounts Settings, fiscal years and multiple companies, the separation of authorities in Role Permissions, the consolidated finance calendar, the handover test, the assembled control map, and the professional boundaries. It opens with the settings walk — performed with the accountant, each answer recorded, re-walked annually.</p><p><b>Open Accounts Settings and walk the fields that matter.</b></p><p>• <b>Accounts Frozen Upto</b> and <b>Role Allowed to Set Frozen Accounts &amp; Edit Frozen Entries</b> — the period lock (module 7). Two health checks: a frozen date that never advances means the lock is not being used; a broadly granted edit role means the lock has no force. Grant the role to two named people at most.<br>• <b>Unlink Payment on Cancellation of Invoice</b> (and its sibling, <b>Unlink Advance Payment on Cancellation of Order</b>) — whether cancelling a document silently unlinks its payment allocations. The wrong value here quietly manufactures module 4's floating-payment backlog on its own. Decide it deliberately with the accountant and record the decision.<br>• <b>Delete Accounting and Stock Ledger Entries on deletion of Transaction</b> — controls what deletion is even capable of. Pair it with deletion rights kept off transaction doctypes for daily roles: module 7's equity pattern is largely made of deletions.<br>• <b>Deferred accounting</b> — <b>Book Deferred Entries Based On</b> and <b>Automatically Process Deferred Accounting Entry</b>: the automation of module 2's matching for item-driven deferred revenue and expense. Enable deliberately, wire its accounts, review its schedules like any template.<br>• <b>Rounding and precision</b> — currency precision consistent between documents and ledgers (a mismatch produces kobo-level reconciliation noise permanently), the Company's Round Off Account pointed at its designated leaf, and rounded-total behaviour set once.<br>• <b>Credit control</b> — the credit-limit machinery's master switches and the bypass role: who may override a blocked document. The gate numbers themselves live on the Customer records.<br>• <b>Naming Series</b> (Settings &gt; Naming Series, for the financial doctypes) — chosen once with a long horizon: the invoice numbers on a filed VAT return must still make sense to an auditor in year seven. Never restarted, never reused.</p><p><b>The walk's three rules.</b></p><p>1. Each field is read as the policy question it is, and the answer is written in the implementation file.<br>2. Changes after go-live are governed events with a reason and a record — not toggles flipped per complaint.<br>3. The annual re-walk (Chapter 4's calendar) is the only routine occasion on which settings change.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Fiscal years and multiple companies", "12", "<p>Two structural mechanics: financial time is partitioned into Fiscal Years, and one site can hold several legal companies.</p><p><b>The Fiscal Year doctype.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Fiscal Year</b>. Each record carries the year's start and end dates and the companies it applies to.</p><p>• <b>Create the next year at least two months early.</b> A January 2nd Sales Invoice that cannot submit because nobody created the year is the most avoidable start-of-year failure.<br>• <b>Align to the statutory year</b> — the calendar year for most Nigerian companies, but the doctype serves any twelve months; the accountant confirms.<br>• <b>Close in sequence</b> — one Period Closing Voucher per year (module 7).<br>• The Fiscal Year is also the <b>Budget's container</b> (module 6) and the axis of the comparative filters on every financial statement — trend reads depend on clean year boundaries.<br>• <b>Backdating into a closed fiscal year</b> changes audited, filed figures. The default answer is module 2's rule: correct in the current period, remarks naming the original date. Formal reopening is the accountant's rare, documented decision.</p><p><b>Multiple companies.</b> The <b>Company</b> doctype supports several legal entities on one site: each with its own chart (or a shared template with per-company accounts), its own Fiscal Year list, its own defaults, its own separately sealed books — with group-level users able to report across.</p><p><b>The rule: companies follow legal reality, never reporting convenience.</b> Separate registered entities keep separate books because the law treats them as separate persons — the audit, the tax filings, and the liability wall all run per entity. A <b>branch is a Cost Center</b> (module 6); a branch promoted to a Company because someone wanted \"separate books\" acquires statutory filing obligations to solve a reporting problem a cost centre solves at no cost.</p><p><b>Inter-company transactions, with the system's machinery.</b> Where entities genuinely transact — management fees, recharges, stock moved, loans:</p><p>1. Mark the counterparties: on the Customer record, <b>Is Internal Customer</b> with its represented company; on the Supplier record, <b>Is Internal Supplier</b>. The system then offers <b>Create Inter Company Invoice</b> — the Sales Invoice in one entity generating the matching Purchase Invoice in the other, references mirrored automatically.<br>2. <b>Reconcile the inter-company balances quarterly:</b> entity A's receivable from B equals B's payable to A, to the kobo.<br>3. Pricing and tax treatment of inter-company charges are the accountant's and tax adviser's territory — set as policy before the first charge.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The four authorities in Role Permissions", "12", "<p>The finance layer has four distinct authorities, and the control design is keeping them in different hands. In the system, the separation is built in the <b>Role Permission Manager</b> and the submission <b>Workflows</b>.</p><p><b>The four authorities, as permissions.</b></p><p>1. <b>Record</b> — create/submit on the transaction doctypes. Trading documents broadly held; the <b>Journal Entry</b> pen narrowly held — module 2's two names, enforced as role permissions on the doctype.<br>2. <b>Release</b> — moving actual money: submit rights on the <b>Payment Entry</b> plus the bank-side mandate. Module 3's rule: the preparer of a run never releases it.<br>3. <b>Close</b> — sealing time: the <b>Role Allowed to Set Frozen Accounts &amp; Edit Frozen Entries</b> (Chapter 1) and the Period Closing Voucher.<br>4. <b>Configure</b> — Accounts Settings, the Chart of Accounts, Cost Centers, dimensions, templates, Workflows: System Manager territory, the narrowest of all. Nobody's routine daily role includes it.</p><p>The rule: <b>the authorities check each other only if they are held by different people.</b> The recorder's entries are paid by someone else's release; the releaser's payments are checked by someone else's reconciliation; the closer's seal is read by someone else's review.</p><p><b>The pairings that must not collapse — what an audit tests first.</b></p><p>• The Journal Entry pen and Payment Entry release in different hands — one person holding both can write any entry and move any money.<br>• Payment preparer and releaser split — two people at any size.<br>• The reconciler is not the sole releaser — the mover of money is not its only checker.<br>• Adjustment powers narrow — the write-off workflow's two names (module 4), the journal Workflow's thresholds (module 2).<br>• The frozen-edit role disjoint from the daily recorders — the person posting today should not be able to repost yesterday's sealed figures.</p><p><b>Sizing.</b> At eleven people, roles share heads: the founder releases what the accountant prepares; the accountant closes what the founder's review checks. The irreducible pairs survive any size because each is two <b>people</b>, not two departments — and where finance is one person, the owner is the second.</p><p><b>The permission matrix.</b> One page: the four authorities mapped to named people, exported from the Role Permission Manager's assignments, reviewed annually and at every personnel change. The review's first question: does any one person hold more than one authority without a documented reason? Chapter 8 shows HealthTrade's answer.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The finance calendar", "12", "<p>The finance function runs on one consolidated calendar. Each line has a named owner and, where the system supports it, a <b>Notification or Auto Repeat</b> behind it so no line depends on memory. The calendar lives on the policy page and is reviewed annually.</p><p><b>Daily.</b></p><p>• The cash position: bank balances and the day's obligations — the Bank Account dashboard and the General Ledger by account.<br>• The exceptions scan: blocked documents, failed payments, till-session variances flagged overnight.<br>• Entry hygiene at the desks: references captured, cost centres cascading — the habits that keep every later routine cheap.</p><p><b>Weekly.</b></p><p>• The bank items sweep: new statement lines imported, charges booked through Create Voucher, deposits matched.<br>• The floating-payments glance: Payment Reconciliation at light cadence — items caught young allocate in minutes.<br>• The payment run cycle where one runs: prepared, evidenced, released by separated hands.</p><p><b>Monthly — the anchor.</b></p><p>• The close checklist (module 7's nine lines), days one to five, containing as collected inputs: the AR/AP routine (module 4's ninety minutes), the tax cycles with their zero checks (module 5), the journal review (module 2), the bank reconciliations (module 3), and the unaddressed check (module 6).<br>• The statements reading with management — the calendar's most important twenty minutes.<br>• The branch P&amp;Ls issued and the Budget Variance Report read (module 6).</p><p><b>Quarterly.</b></p><p>• The provision review in depth — the matrix's judgments revisited (module 4).<br>• The correction log read by cause — upstream faults surfaced (module 2).<br>• The bank-charges read against the agreed schedule (module 3).<br>• The allocation pool audit — direct-first shrinking it (module 6).<br>• The advance agings' escalations resolved (module 4).<br>• Inter-company reconciliations where the structure exists (Chapter 2).</p><p><b>Annually — scheduled across weeks, not stacked.</b></p><p>• The Accounts Settings walk (Chapter 1) and the permission matrix review (Chapter 3).<br>• The chart walk — every account explainable in one sentence (module 1).<br>• The dimension audit (module 6) and the bank mandate review (module 3).<br>• The capitalisation policy review and the Fixed Asset Register verification (module 7).<br>• The budget build (module 6 — November) and the next Fiscal Year created (Chapter 2).<br>• The compliance calendar re-confirmed against current law (module 5).<br>• The year-end mechanics and the Period Closing Voucher (module 7).</p><p>Practical rule: a calendar line without a named owner will stop running. The annual review's first task is confirming every line still has one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The finance handover test", "12", "<p>The handover test asks one question: could a competent newcomer, given only what is documented, run the finance function for a month with zero questions that only the departing person can answer? Finance — the function most often held in one trusted head — needs the test most.</p><p><b>The test, walked module by module.</b> A finance function passes when:</p><p>1. The <b>chart</b> is documented — every account with its one-sentence purpose (module 1).<br>2. The <b>journal governance</b> is written: the pen's two names in Role Permissions, the template list, the correction rules (module 2).<br>3. The <b>bank layer</b> is documented: every Bank Account record with its purpose, every Mode of Payment mapping, the mandate — with last month's Bank Reconciliation Statements showing where each uncleared item stands (module 3).<br>4. The <b>AR/AP routine's</b> checklist and policy pages state the tie check, the provision matrix, and the write-off workflow (module 4).<br>5. The <b>compliance calendar</b> lists every obligation, day, authority, and evidence folder — with twelve months of folders showing the pattern (module 5).<br>6. The <b>cost centre design</b> is documented and the allocation policy answers every sharing question by pointing (module 6).<br>7. The <b>close checklist</b> states the month's nine lines, and the statement-reading method is written (module 7).<br>8. This module's <b>calendar, permission matrix, and settings record</b> hold the structure.</p><p>Every \"no\" identifies a module incompletely done. The documentation is not an extra deliverable — it is the by-product of each module done properly.</p><p><b>The absence test.</b> The practical version: the key person takes uninterrupted leave, and the function runs on its calendar. What breaks during the absence is exactly what the documentation is missing. Run deliberately, it is the cheapest structural audit available, and its findings are fixed without a crisis.</p><p><b>The role path.</b> A documented function makes progression legible: the bookkeeper (entries and desk hygiene) grows into the accountant (the close's owner, the journal pen's holder, the statements' first reader), who grows into the controller (the calendar's owner, the policies' custodian, the management reading's co-chair). Clean closes, young uncleared lists, and tied bridges are a promotion case written in numbers. The alternative — the whole function in one trusted head — is a single point of failure regardless of skill, and Chapter 9 treats it as one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The control map: preventive and detective", "12", "<p>The track installed controls one module at a time. Assembled, they form a map with two families — and the assembly check is that every control in one family has a partner in the other.</p><p><b>Preventive controls — stop it before it posts.</b></p><p>• <b>Separations:</b> the four authorities in Role Permissions; the two-names patterns (journal pen, write-off, frozen-edit).<br>• <b>Validations:</b> mandatory Party on Receivable/Payable accounts; Mandatory For Profit and Loss on dimensions; the Journal Entry Difference field; the Stock-account fence.<br>• <b>Fences:</b> the frozen period (Accounts Frozen Upto); masters-not-messages for beneficiary changes; the forbidden journal list.<br>• <b>Gates:</b> Workflow approval thresholds; credit limits; Budget Stop actions on the governance account families.</p><p><b>Detective controls — catch what slipped through.</b></p><p>• <b>Reconciliations:</b> the Bank Reconciliation Tool monthly; the supplier-statement mirror.<br>• <b>Ties:</b> the control-to-report tie check; the register-to-account bridges; the custody zero check.<br>• <b>Reviews:</b> the monthly journal review; the Trial Balance scan; the audit-adjustments read.<br>• <b>Aged reads:</b> the uncleared-items list; the floating-payments list; the correction log by cause.</p><p><b>The pairing principle.</b> Journal governance prevents; the journal review detects. The release separation prevents; the bank reconciliation detects. Mandatory dimensions prevent; the unaddressed check detects. A map with an unpaired fence has a blind spot — walk the map once and name each control's partner.</p><p><b>The fraud triangle, briefly.</b> Fraud needs opportunity, pressure, and rationalisation. The business controls only <b>opportunity</b>, which is what the preventive family shrinks: the everything-role dismantled, custody segregated, the pen narrowed, the masters guarded. The detective family shortens time-to-discovery: what would compound for years surfaces at the month's reconciliation. The framing taught throughout removes the easiest rationalisation: controls protect the honest holder — the clean count and the documented approval are the honest person's own evidence.</p><p><b>The control self-assessment (CSA), annually.</b></p><p>1. List the controls by family, one page per control.<br>2. For each, name the evidence of its last execution: the reconciliation statement, the review sign-off, the Workflow approval log.<br>3. Read the tell-tale numbers: approval times (a second signature granted in seconds is not a review), uncleared ages, sweep sizes.<br>4. Revive or formally retire dead controls. A designed-but-dead control is worse than an absent one, because someone is relying on it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Professional boundaries", "12", "<p>The finance layer is run alongside three professionals, and the boundaries must be held clearly — clients will blur them monthly.</p><p><b>The boundary map.</b></p><p>• <b>The system role</b> — the person who configures and operates ZhiftERP (this certification's territory) — owns the machinery: the configuration, the templates, the registers, the routines, the evidence structure, and the literacy to walk any entry with any professional as a peer. It does not make policy judgments.<br>• <b>The accountant</b> owns the policy: method choices, the provision matrix's percentages, the capitalisation threshold, recognition schedules, reopening decisions, and professional responsibility for the statements.<br>• <b>The tax adviser</b> owns the positions: rates, eligibility, reliefs, and stances where the law is unclear (module 5's boundary).<br>• <b>The auditor</b> owns the opinion: independent, sample-based, and best served by being served organised evidence (module 7's file).</p><p>The dividing sentence, worth memorising: <b>the machinery makes the professionals' judgments executable and evidenced; it does not make the judgments.</b></p><p><b>The four working patterns.</b></p><p>1. <b>The accountant co-designs from day one.</b> The chart walk, the posting-map verification, the settings walk, and the policy pages are done with the accountant in the room. An accounts layer built without the accountant who must own it gets built twice.<br>2. <b>Habit conflicts resolve by demonstration, not authority.</b> Where the system's discipline conflicts with an accountant's established habit — the journal-habit case of module 2 is the standard example — the resolution is showing the document-first flow working and transferring the fluency, never deriding it.<br>3. <b>The auditor is engaged early.</b> The interim walk-through invited rather than endured, the close-bundle structure shared in advance, adjustments booked by the correction rules. A clean first year buys a lighter second — the relationship compounds like the authority relationship does.<br>4. <b>The tax adviser is consulted before configuration.</b> The new revenue stream's VAT treatment, inter-company pricing, and reform eligibility are settled before the templates are built. Reconfiguring after a wrong position costs far more than the consultation.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Machinery, policy, positions, opinion — four owners. Hold the boundary, co-design with the accountant, meet the auditor early, and loop the tax adviser in before configuring anything the law touches.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's structural year", "13", "<p>The structural layer, installed over four quarters. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>Q1 — the settings walk (Chapters 1-2).</b> The walk runs with the accountant, field by field, answers recorded. <b>Accounts Frozen Upto</b> is configured with the two-name edit role, and the freeze advance joins the close checklist. The <b>Unlink Payment on Cancellation of Invoice</b> setting is found at its wrong value — it had been silently unlinking allocations at every invoice cancellation, which explained part of module 4's original floating-payment backlog — and corrected, with the decision recorded. Naming Series are confirmed for the long term. The next <b>Fiscal Year</b> is created two months early, closing off the January failure before it can occur.</p><p><b>Q2 — the permission matrix (Chapter 3).</b> The review exports the role assignments and finds the implementation-era all-permissions role still live on one user: the accountant, holding record, release, close, and configure \"temporarily\" since go-live. It is dismantled as a design exercise, not an accusation: Journal Entry create/submit narrowed to the accountant plus the named backstop, Payment Entry release moved to the founder (the founder releases what the accountant prepares), the frozen-edit role confirmed at two names, System Manager removed from daily use, and the matrix documented on one page. At the next audit, the walk-through passes its separation tests for the first time.</p><p><b>Q3 — the control self-assessment (Chapter 6).</b> The first CSA walks the map by family and finds one designed-but-dead control: the second signature on large Journal Entries — live in the Workflow, rubber-stamped in practice, approvals landing in seconds, unread. It is revived with an evidence habit: the approver samples the entry's references before signing, and approval times are read quarterly from the Workflow log. The pairing walk confirms every fence has its detective partner, and the one-page-per-control file is started.</p><p><b>Q4 — the absence test (Chapter 5).</b> The founder takes three uninterrupted weeks. Finance runs on its calendar: the close lands on day five without the founder's review, the payment runs release under the deputy-mandate design, and the compliance calendar's two in-window filings land on their days with their folders. The handover walk afterwards scores its first clean sheet — zero founder-only questions in the finance function. The MD's note in the year file: <i>the business now runs on its documents in every function, including the one that counts them.</i></p><p><b>The year in numbers.</b> Close: day five every month of H2. Audit: walk-through passed, three agreed adjustments, fee reduced. Floating payments: near zero every month since the unlink fix. Permission matrix: four authorities, no undocumented overlaps. Absence test: passed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes, the eighth law & the track's laws", "12", "<p>The structural layer's recurring failures, the module's law, and the track's eight laws assembled.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — settings changed casually.</b> Symptom: the frozen date never advancing; the unlink setting flipped per complaint; precision changed mid-year. Cause: Accounts Settings treated as toggles instead of policy (Chapter 1). Fix: settings behind the Configure authority, changes as governed events with a record, and the annual walk as the only routine occasion for change.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the everything-role.</b> Symptom: one user holding record, release, close, and configure; the audit walk-through's first finding, every time. Cause: setup convenience never dismantled (Chapter 3). Fix: the four authorities separated in Role Permissions by the two-people patterns, the matrix documented and reviewed annually, and the dismantling run as a design exercise, not an accusation.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the branch promoted to a company.</b> Symptom: statutory filings multiplying; inter-company balances nobody reconciles; \"separate books\" a Cost Center would have provided free. Cause: companies created for reporting convenience instead of legal reality (Chapter 2). Fix: cost centres for reading, Companies for legal persons, and the wrongly created entity wound down or made legally real with the accountant.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — designed-but-dead controls.</b> Symptom: reconciliations that quietly stopped; second signatures granted in seconds; checklist lines dropped. Cause: false assurance (Chapter 6) — worse than absence, because someone relies on the control. Fix: the annual CSA on evidence, the approval-time and uncleared-age numbers read, and dead controls revived or formally retired.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the single-person function.</b> Symptom: finance running entirely in one trusted head; leave never taken; a close only one person can perform; an owner who cannot read the statements unaided. Cause: competence without structure (Chapter 5). Fix: the handover test as the standard, the documentation as each module's by-product, the role path making structure a promotion engine, and the absence test run deliberately.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: walk the Accounts Settings fields — the freeze pair, the unlink settings, deletion, deferred accounting, rounding, naming series — and state the three walk rules; create Fiscal Years ahead and state the companies-follow-legal-reality rule with the Is Internal Customer/Supplier machinery; map the four authorities in Role Permissions and name the pairings an audit tests; recite the calendar by frequency with its Notifications; run the handover and absence tests; walk the control map's two families with the pairing principle and the CSA; and state the four professional boundaries.</p><p><b>The eighth law: structure over individuals — authorities separated, settings governed, rhythms on the calendar, and the function passes the handover test.</b></p><p><b>The track's eight laws.</b></p><p>1. The books are written by documents, into a chart designed for reading.<br>2. The journal is the exception and the document is the rule — what a document can do, a document must do.<br>3. Every bank account is reconciled monthly, and every difference is named — never plugged.<br>4. The record is kept real, collectable, current and complete.<br>5. Tax money is held, not owned — reconciled, filed from the system, remitted from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.<br>6. One chart, many readings — every posting carries its address, contribution decides, and allocation is written.<br>7. The books close monthly, the statements are read monthly, and a sealed month stays sealed.<br>8. Structure over individuals — authorities separated, settings governed, rhythms on the calendar, and the function passes the handover test.</p><p>This completes the Accounts Professional track.</p>"),
]

